<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guntram</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 532–592</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Saint Guntram, Gontran · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merovingian-dynasty">Merovingian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Guntram was a Merovingian king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> who ruled the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> from 561 to 592. A son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chlothar-i">Chlothar I</a>, he received Burgundy with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a> when the kingdom was divided among the brothers, and he ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalon-sur-saone">Chalon-sur-Saône</a>. He fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bretons">Bretons</a> and his brothers' kingdoms and was later venerated as a saint.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guntram.jpg" alt="Guntram" width="320" height="157" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Atelier de Chalon-sur-Saône · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tiers_de_sou_de_Gontran_frapp%C3%A9_%C3%A0_Chalon-sur-Sa%C3%B4ne.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Guntram was born about 532 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soissons">Soissons</a>, the third-eldest and second-eldest surviving son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chlothar-i">Chlothar I</a> and Ingunda. On his father's death in 561 he became king of a fourth of the Kingdom of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>. After his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charibert-i">Charibert I</a> died in 567, the surviving brothers, Guntram, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigebert-i">Sigebert I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chilperic-i">Chilperic I</a>, shared his lands, at first holding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in common. Charibert's widow Theudechild proposed marriage to Guntram, but he lodged her in a monastery at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arles">Arles</a> instead.</p><p>In 573 he was drawn into civil war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigebert-i">Sigebert I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrasia">Austrasia</a> and in 575 called on Chilperic for help, but changed sides and remained Sigebert's ally. When Sigebert was assassinated in 575 and Chilperic invaded, Guntram sent his general Mummolus, who defeated Chilperic's general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/desiderius">Desiderius</a>. After his two surviving sons died of dysentery in 577, he adopted his nephew Childebert II, Sigebert's son, as heir.</p><p>About 584 or 585 a man named Gundowald, claiming to be an illegitimate son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chlothar-i">Chlothar I</a>, took cities including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>; Guntram besieged him, and his followers gave him up for execution. In 587 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fredegund">Fredegund</a> attempted to have Guntram assassinated, without success. That year he concluded the Treaty of Andelot with Childebert, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunhilda-of-austrasia">Brunhilda</a> and others, which lasted until his death. He also compelled the Breton ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waroch-ii">Waroch II</a> to renew his oath, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bretons">Bretons</a> stayed independent. In 590 he put down a rebellion of his niece Basina at the Holy Cross abbey in Poitiers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-tours">Gregory of Tours</a>, the chief chronicler of the period, calls him the good king Guntram. He describes a man who after a period of intemperance repented, fasted and prayed, protected the oppressed, cared for the sick, gave generously in plague and famine, and built and endowed churches and monasteries. Guntram died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalon-sur-saone">Chalon-sur-Saône</a> on 28 March 592 and was succeeded by Childebert II. He was buried in the Church of Saint Marcellus, which he had founded. He was soon proclaimed a saint, and his feast day is 28 March.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Guntram” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Waroch repeatedly raided Frankish territory around Rennes and Nantes and defeated or bought off Frankish armies sent by <b>Guntram</b> and Chilperic I in the 570s and 580s.</blockquote><a href="/state/broerec">Broërec</a></li><li><blockquote>At the division of 561 Chlothar I's son <b>Guntram</b> received Burgundy with Orléans, ruling from Chalon-sur-Saône.</blockquote><a href="/state/frankish-kingdom-of-burgundy">Frankish Kingdom of Burgundy</a></li><li><blockquote>Salem was founded in the 1130s by the knight <b>Guntram</b> of Adelsreute as a Cistercian house settled by monks from Lucelle.</blockquote><a href="/state/imperial-abbey-of-salem">Imperial Abbey of Salem</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soissons">Soissons</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalon-sur-saone">Chalon-sur-Saône</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.38,3.32&amp;year=571" aria-label="Soissons on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4626 442 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Guntram"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5076 712h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5076 712h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.38,3.32&amp;year=571">What was here in 571 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
